# 🧪 Standard Operating Procedure: Sociable Unit Tests

**Status:** Mandatory  
**Version:** 1.1

**NON-NEGOTIABLE STANDARD:** Adherence to this SOP is mandatory for all Sociable Unit Tests. Deviations are not permitted without explicit architectural review and approval.

## 📌 Purpose

This SOP defines the standards for writing Sociable Unit Tests for the application layer of the Digital Kudos Wall frontend. Its primary goal is to ensure that our **Use Cases** are tested effectively, verifying their business logic and behavior.

---

## Scope

This standard applies exclusively to tests for **Use Case classes** located in `src/features/{feature-name}/application/use-cases/`. It does NOT apply to custom hooks.

## Definitions

- **Sociable Unit Test:** A test that verifies the **business behavior** of a Use Case class. It uses real domain objects (Entities, Value Objects) but mocks dependencies at the I/O boundary (e.g., `AuthService`, `HttpClient`).
- **Unit:** A single Use Case class.

## Key Principles (Non-Negotiable)

- **Behavior-Driven:** Tests MUST verify the observable business outcome of the `execute` method. They should focus on what the use case _does_, not _how_ it does it.
- **State, Not Interactions:** Assertions MUST focus on the returned result or errors thrown. Interaction testing (`toHaveBeenCalledWith`) should be avoided.
- **Real Collaborators:** Use real instances of internal domain objects.
- **Test Doubles for Boundaries:** Mock interfaces to external systems or I/O.
- **Speed and Determinism:** Tests must be fast and produce the same result every time.

## Test Structure (Arrange-Act-Assert)

All tests MUST follow the Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern and focus on behavioral outcomes.

```typescript
// Example: src/features/auth/application/use-cases/register-user/RegisterUserUseCase.test.ts
import { RegisterUserUseCase } from "./RegisterUserUseCase";
import { AuthService, RegisterUserPayload } from "../../../interfaces/AuthService";
import { User } from "../../../types/User";

describe("Sociable Unit Test: RegisterUserUseCase", () => {
  let useCase: RegisterUserUseCase;
  let mockAuthService: jest.Mocked<AuthService>;

  beforeEach(() => {
    mockAuthService = {
      registerUser: jest.fn(),
      login: jest.fn(),
    };
    useCase = new RegisterUserUseCase(mockAuthService);
  });

  it("should successfully register a valid user and return the user object", async () => {
    // Arrange
    const payload: RegisterUserPayload = { name: "Jane Doe", email: "jane@example.com", password: "Password123!" };
    const expectedUser: User = { id: "user-456", name: "Jane Doe", email: "jane@example.com" };
    mockAuthService.registerUser.mockResolvedValue(expectedUser);

    // Act
    const result = await useCase.execute(payload);

    // Assert (State-focused)
    expect(result).toEqual(expectedUser);
    expect(result.name).toBe("Jane Doe");
  });

  it("should throw an error if registration fails", async () => {
    // Arrange
    const payload: RegisterUserPayload = { name: "Jane Doe", email: "jane@example.com", password: "Password123!" };
    mockAuthService.registerUser.mockRejectedValue(new Error("Email already exists"));

    // Act & Assert (Behavior-focused)
    await expect(useCase.execute(payload)).rejects.toThrow("Email already exists");
  });
});
```

## Example Implementation

- **Canonical Example:** `src/features/auth/application/use-cases/register-user/RegisterUserUseCase.test.ts`

## Enforcement

Violation of these standards will result in build failures during CI and/or rejection during code review. These standards are non-negotiable to maintain the integrity and effectiveness of our testing suite.
